{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"fix-ssrf-vulnerability","kind":"prompt","title":"Fix server-side request forgery (SSRF)","description":"Hardens features that fetch user-supplied URLs, such as webhooks, link previews and image import, with allowlists, resolved-IP checks, blocked metadata ranges and redirect limits.","category":"security","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["build","verify"],"role":["backend-engineer","software-engineer","security-engineer"],"stack":[],"requires":["repo-read","file-write","shell"],"inputs":["repo","file","text"],"output":["diff","tests"],"risk":"runs-commands","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"autonomous","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"expert","depth":"advanced","tags":["ssrf","cloud-metadata","url-validation","owasp"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["audit-input-handling","build-webhook-handler","review-cloud-iam-policy","write-exploit-regression-test"],"personas":["security-auditor"]},"args":[{"name":"feature","description":"The feature or code that fetches URLs on the server, and who can supply them.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"stack","description":"Language, HTTP client and where it runs (cloud provider, Kubernetes). Leave empty to detect it from the repo.","type":"string"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Fetch points","Design","Changes","Tests","Verification","Residual risk"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nAny server feature that fetches a URL a user chose can be turned against the network behind it: cloud metadata endpoints (`169.254.169.254`, `fd00:ec2::254`, `metadata.google.internal`) hand out credentials, and internal admin services trust the server's address. Checking the hostname string does not work. Attackers use redirects to internal addresses, DNS names that resolve to private IPs, DNS rebinding (a name that resolves to a public IP at check time and a private one at fetch time), and alternate IP spellings (decimal `2130706433`, octal, IPv6-mapped `::ffff:127.0.0.1`, `0.0.0.0`). The reliable fix resolves the name once, checks every resolved address against blocked ranges, connects to that exact address, and repeats the check on every redirect hop. The strongest layer is network-level: run fetches from an egress proxy or isolated worker that cannot reach internal ranges, and require IMDSv2 or the provider's equivalent so metadata needs a header the attacker cannot set.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFix SSRF in {{feature}}.\n{{#stack}}Stack: {{stack}}{{/stack}}\n\n1. Find every place the server fetches a URL or connects to a host that came from users, tenants or stored data: webhooks, link previews, image or file import by URL, PDF or screenshot renderers, OAuth or OpenID discovery URLs, XML or SVG processors that load external resources, and integrations with a configurable base URL.\n2. Decide per fetch point whether an allowlist of hosts is possible (integrations with known providers) or the feature must reach the open internet (webhooks, previews).\n3. Implement one shared safe-fetch helper used by every fetch point:\n   - allow only `http` and `https`, and only standard ports unless the feature needs others;\n   - resolve the hostname, reject if any address is loopback, private, link-local, carrier-grade NAT, multicast, unspecified, IPv6 unique-local or mapped forms of these, or a metadata address;\n   - connect to the checked IP (pin it in the HTTP client) so a second DNS lookup cannot change it;\n   - follow redirects manually, re-running the checks on each hop, with a small hop limit;\n   - set connect and total timeouts and a response size cap, and do not return raw error bodies or internal timing to the user.\n4. Recommend the network layer for {{feature}}: an egress proxy or isolated worker, and metadata protection (IMDSv2 with hop limit 1, or the provider's equivalent).\n5. Write tests for the helper: private and metadata IPs, alternate IP encodings, a public name that resolves to a private IP, a redirect to an internal address, a non-HTTP scheme, and an oversized response. Use a stub resolver and a local server; no real external calls.\n6. Run the tests and the linter.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never rely on string matching of the hostname or a blocklist of hostnames.\n- Do not test against real cloud metadata endpoints or other people's systems.\n- Keep legitimate destinations working; list any customer-visible change, such as webhooks to private IPs no longer being allowed.\n- Do not change cloud or network configuration yourself; describe it.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Fetch points\nTable: file:line, feature, who controls the URL, allowlist possible (yes or no), status.\n## Design\nThe safe-fetch rules chosen and why, in a short list.\n## Changes\nOne line per file: `path`, what changed.\n## Tests\nOne line per test.\n## Verification\nCommands run and their actual results.\n## Residual risk\nWhat code alone cannot stop, and the network or cloud controls that would.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
